from pathlib import Path
import os, re, hashlib, json
root = Path(os.environ['REVIEW_ROOT'])
changed = set()
def replace(path, old, new):
    p = root/path; s = p.read_text()
    if old not in s: raise RuntimeError('Missing text in '+path+': '+old[:80])
    p.write_text(s.replace(old,new)); changed.add(path)
def paragraph(path, start, text):
    p=root/path; s=p.read_text(); matches=[x for x in (s.splitlines() if start.startswith('- ') else s.split('\n\n')) if x.startswith(start)]
    if len(matches)!=1: raise RuntimeError('Ambiguous paragraph '+path+': '+start)
    p.write_text(s.replace(matches[0],text)); changed.add(path)
def append(path, text):
    p=root/path; p.write_text(p.read_text().rstrip()+'\n\n'+text.strip()+'\n'); changed.add(path)
def after_title(path,text):
    p=root/path; s=p.read_text(); a,b=s.split('\n',1);p.write_text(a+'\n\n'+text.strip()+'\n'+b); changed.add(path)

paragraph('docs/architecture.md','The application processes fixed one-simulation-second steps,', '''The application advances boundary-limited elapsed intervals, not one transition per game second. The existing 50 ms host wake admits time debt; routine durability remains coalesced to one real second and commands/control changes commit immediately. Clean shutdown flushes pending progress; an abrupt crash can lose only the already documented unflushed routine progress. Each call reconciles due start-state decisions and status/action transitions, captures active rates, forecasts a bounded interval, integrates that elapsed time, then commits completions and perception before reconsidering. New work receives no preceding time. `temporal-boundaries.ts` composes the current finite families; `simulation-time.ts` supplies shared rate/condition calculations. The base-world [time policy](worlds/base/time.md) owns fidelity and fallback values; [simulation time](simulation-time.md) owns the contract.

The host subtracts actual `simTime` progress, not requested steps, and yields mutation ownership between approximately eight-millisecond batches. One atomic interval can exceed that soft budget. The base conversion remains sixty game seconds per wall second at 1x; speed increases owed time, not an update count. Navigation preparation, genuine suspension and explicit pause remain separately accounted. Speed changes retain admitted debt; explicit pause/suspension follow their existing reset policy. Rendering and camera input remain device-paced; a possible twenty-Hz pose publication policy is not a twenty-FPS rendering limit.

Domain transitions use isolated Immer drafts with unchanged branches shared. The server freezes loaded/migrated state, accepted command/editor snapshots and native interval results, skipping already-frozen branches. Builders remain mutable until server handoff; auto-freezing remains disabled. Domain callers must not mutate returned snapshots. Selected rates, phase-local IDs and working-action identity are captured before integration. This is not a general event scheduler: one global minimum interval still couples dense local activity to unrelated participants, sensing uses a documented displacement bound, and arbitrary coupled/clamped rules retain a conservative fallback. [PF13](maintainers/performance.md#pf13--elapsed-time-simulation) distinguishes delivery from extreme-scale qualification.''')
